\viewkind4\uc1 \f1\fs20\b WORKSHEET\b0\par
\f1\fs32\b The God who remembers and calls\b0\fs24\par
\f1\fs20 Torah: Genesis 21:1\u8211?34 | Haftarah: 1 Samuel 1:1\u8211?2:10 | Gospel: Matthew 24:29\u8211?36\par
\f0\fs22\i On Yom Teruah God remembers: He visits Sarah (Gen. 21:1) and remembers Hannah (1 Sam. 1:19) \u8212? the classic reason these very texts are the day's readings. The trumpet blast wakes us to hear: what we received is entrusted to us and carries a calling; giving back is the wakeful act of one who knows from whom it came.\i0\par
\par\f0\fs24 
\par\b\fs28 How to use this sheet\b0\fs24\par


\par Work from top to bottom: first observe (A1), then weigh (A2), then diagnose (A3), and only then summarise (A4). The direction \u8212? receiving or waked and called \u8212? is the horizon, not the starting point.\par


\par\b\fs28 Basic data\b0\fs24\par


\par Parashah / scope: Yom Teruah \u8212? Genesis 21:1\u8211?34. Haftarah: 1 Samuel 1:1\u8211?2:10. Flanking reading and status: Matthew 24:29\u8211?36 \u8212? New Testament reflection, used confessionally (not as peshat). Audience: study group / congregation.\par



